In article <6k1Lc.41765$5Y.26329@cyclops.nntpserver.com>, "Richard Tomkins" <tomkinsr@istop.com> writes:

:Good reply. I never sat down to actually work out the time required to write
:every bit 5 million times, but I had had some SCSI Flash drives myself at
:one time. I bougth 5 for $10.00 each, 20MB each and SCSI too.

  We are working with rather larger flash disks; disks up in the one to 
  two gigabyte capacity range.   These disks cost rather more than your
  disks, too.  

  The small USB flash disks are sometimes seen as "gimmes" at trade shows,
  but I can't say I've seen a SCSI flash disk "gimme"...

  If the (re)write limits are per-bit, that's comparatively less painful
  as OpenVMS doesn't tend to update in place all that regularly.  (The UAF
  and some other core files, obviously, will see these I/O block rewrites,
  of course, but to my knowledge we do not "pound" on specific disk blocks.
  Well, come to think of it, we do write the quorum file very regularly,
  and might well hit some of the other volume structure files regularly.)
  If the (re)write limits are device-wide -- (re)writes occuring anywhere
  across the semiconductor flash device, in other words -- then that makes
  for a rather shorter aggregate device lifetime.

