>   If the (re)write limits are per-bit, that's comparatively less painful
>   as OpenVMS doesn't tend to update in place all that regularly.  (The UAF
>   and some other core files, obviously, will see these I/O block rewrites,
>   of course, but to my knowledge we do not "pound" on specific disk blocks.
>   Well, come to think of it, we do write the quorum file very regularly,
>   and might well hit some of the other volume structure files regularly.)
>   If the (re)write limits are device-wide -- (re)writes occuring anywhere
>   across the semiconductor flash device, in other words -- then that makes
>   for a rather shorter aggregate device lifetime.

As I understand it, the write limitation is per bit.

The problem is that the rewriting of a bit can degrade the insulation
surrounding the cell slightly.  Rewriting the bit often enough can "burn
through", resulting in a bit that will no longer hold a charge.

The rewrite limit is a minimum guaranteed performance spec for all bits
in the device.  Actual performance may be orders of magnitude higher,
but all you need is for a single bit to fail in an unfortunate place.

Hmm...  I suppose that if a bit burned through, you could use ANAL/MEDIA
to remap the block. (If you could get it to work on the device.) 
Alternately, if the device supports revectoring, it should be possible
to survice many bit failures.

